From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noxwell <beebono@yahoo.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 13:03:31 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 198/225] arm64: dts: ums512: make thermal throttling actually
 work

Three separate faults kept the SoC from ever throttling; under an all-core
load the die reached 119C and tripped the 110C critical shutdown, which is
what the "random" reboots were.

1. Thermal zones never polled. Every zone had polling-delay-passive but no
   polling-delay, which defaults to 0 = never poll. sprd_thermal registers
   only .get_temp - no .set_trips, no irq - so the zone is poll-only and was
   never updated: the governor never ran and cooling_device cur_state sat at
   0 while the die climbed. polling-delay-passive is unreachable on its own,
   since a zone cannot enter passive mode without a first update to notice
   the trip. Stock has polling-delay on all 15 zones; we had it on 1 of 12.
   Also give gpu-thermal trips and a cooling map (it was sensor-only) and
   the #cooling-cells it needs to be referenced.

2. Sensor trim was noise. The cells lived under a cache_efuse@800 rmem
   shadow that the bootloader is supposed to populate and ours does not, so
   all 12 sensors got garbage calibration and read up to 66C apart. Read the
   fuses directly via the real controller instead. Cell offsets are the
   vendor ones verbatim with no +0x4: that fold was an artifact of the cache
   layout, and sprd-efuse adds SPRD_EFUSE_NORMAL_BLOCK_OFFSET itself, so
   nvmem offset 0 is normal block 0 either way. Verified against a measured
   23.9C ambient after a cold soak - all 12 zones now agree within 2.7C.

3. Governor could not do its job. power_allocator was the only governor
   built, and it needs sustainable-power, which no zone declares. Stock runs
   IPA against a single aggregated soc-thmzone with a tuned budget; we have
   12 independent zones, 8 of which bind the same CPU0 cooling device. Build
   and default to step_wise, which needs no power model. IPA stays available
   per-zone via sysfs.

Throttling now engages and peaks dropped from 119C to 73C.

Separately, throttle by injecting idle rather than scaling frequency. The
vendor hw dvfs block accepts a work index (the register holds the write) but
never applies it: state stays IDLE, voted_volt reads 0, and sel/div never
leave the values u-boot left. openssl throughput and battery draw are
identical at 614400 and 2002000 (2.68W vs 2.71W active), so the cpufreq
cooling devices are inert here and the die still climbed 30C at cur_state
9/9. cpuidle cooling does not need working clock control. One thermal-idle
node covers the whole cpuidle driver cpumask, so a node per cpu would only
create duplicates contending with each other.

Note for anyone revisiting the hw dvfs path: the voltage-grade values in
ums512-rg-rotate.dts are generic placeholders and do NOT match this board.
Hardware is correctly programmed (by the bootloader) with the real per-OPP
voltages - grades decode to 756250..1050000uV at 3.125mV/step, exactly the
cpufreq_clus0 table. Programming the DT values over them would undervolt.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
